-- ============================================================================-- STEP 21 · the privilege layer the phases never touched: TRUNCATE / DELETE / INSERT on public.profiles-- ============================================================================-- Found by the phase-11 verification, not by this file's author: production reported-- `app_grants_on_profiles = DELETE INSERT MAINTAIN REFERENCES TRIGGER TRUNCATE` for `authenticated`.-- The absence of UPDATE is the phase-1 + phase-10 work, and it is real. What IS present is Supabase's default-- `grant all on all tables in schema public to authenticated`, which the hardening phases narrowed column-wise for-- SELECT/UPDATE but never revoked wholesale — and TRUNCATE is the one privilege no row policy can stop, because it-- is a table statement, not a row statement. Privileges are checked before RLS.---- Scope is deliberately narrow: only public.profiles, the one table that holds every account. Revoking across-- `all tables in schema public` would look tidier and would break the app, because phase 1's write policies DO let-- `authenticated` insert into match_interest and read notification_devices — those inserts currently ride on the-- default grant, so a broad revoke converts a security improvement into an outage.---- What is NOT revoked and why:--   SELECT  — the row policy + RLS define which rows; a fan reading other fans' public columns is the product.--   UPDATE  — phase 1 revoked it and phase 10 replaced it with the kicklive_profile_update writer; whatever this--             project's history left, this file does not re-grant it and does not need to touch it.-- Effect on the app, asserted below rather than assumed: the sign-up trigger is SECURITY DEFINER (it writes as its-- owner, so it does not need INSERT), and the admin writer does the same, so removing INSERT/DELETE from the API-- roles removes a capability nothing uses.-- ============================================================================begin;-- ── 1 · what the API roles actually hold on the account table, right now ────────────────────────────────select 'public.profiles' as object,       (select r.rolname from pg_class c join pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace          join pg_roles r on r.oid = c.relowner         where n.nspname = 'public' and c.relname = 'profiles')           as owner,       (select relrowsecurity from pg_class c join pg_namespace n on n.oid = c.relnamespace         where n.nspname = 'public' and c.relname = 'profiles')           as rls_on,       has_table_privilege('authenticated', 'public.profiles', 'SELECT')    as auth_select,       has_table_privilege('authenticated', 'public.profiles', 'UPDATE')    as auth_update,       has_table_privilege('authenticated', 'public.profiles', 'INSERT')    as auth_insert,       has_table_privilege('authenticated', 'public.profiles', 'DELETE')    as auth_delete,       has_table_privilege('authenticated', 'public.profiles', 'TRUNCATE')  as auth_truncate,       has_table_privilege('authenticated', 'public.profiles', 'REFERENCES') as auth_references,       has_table_privilege('authenticated', 'public.profiles', 'TRIGGER')   as auth_trigger,       has_table_privilege('anon', 'public.profiles', 'INSERT')            as anon_insert,       has_table_privilege('anon', 'public.profiles', 'TRUNCATE')          as anon_truncate;-- ── 1b · the same hole, elsewhere: how many tables can the API roles TRUNCATE right now?-- Read the count, do not act on it inside this paste. A schema-wide revoke is the correct eventual answer and the-- wrong *unverified* one, because phase 1's write policies (match_interest inserts, notification_devices reads)-- ride on Supabase's default `grant all`, so `revoke … on all tables in schema public` removes those inserts and-- reads too. If the count below is large, decide it deliberately:--   revoke truncate, trigger, references on all tables in schema public from authenticated, anon;-- then run `npm run check:sql` (scripts/sql-flow.mjs